1. What is a Subnet Mask?

A Subnet Mask tells the computer:

Which part of the IP address represents the network
Which part represents the host (device)

The most common subnet mask is:

255.255.255.0

2. Easy Example

Suppose your IP address is:

192.168.1.25

Subnet Mask:

255.255.255.0

This means:

Network Part:
192.168.1

Host Part:
25

Every device whose IP starts with:

192.168.1

belongs to the same network.

Examples:

192.168.1.5
192.168.1.25
192.168.1.100

These devices can communicate directly.

3. Real-Life Example

Think of an apartment building.

Building Number = Network

Flat Number = Host

Example:

Building 12
Flat 101

Building 12
Flat 205

Both flats are in the same building (network).

4. CIDR Notation

CIDR stands for:

Classless Inter-Domain Routing

Instead of writing:

255.255.255.0

we write:

/24

So,

192.168.1.0/24

means:

Network = 24 bits
Host = 8 bits

==> Common CIDR values 

| CIDR | Subnet Mask       |
| ---- | ----------------- |
| /8   | 255.0.0.0         |
| /16  | 255.255.0.0       |
| /24  | 255.255.255.0     |
| /32  | Single IP Address |


==> AWS Example

When creating a VPC, you might choose:

10.0.0.0/16

Inside that VPC, you create subnets like:

10.0.1.0/24
10.0.2.0/24

This divides the large network into smaller networks.

==> Useful Linux Commands

Check IP address:

ip addr

Show routing table:

ip route

Display IP addresses only:

hostname -I

Test connectivity:

ping 8.8.8.8

Check DNS resolution:

nslookup google.com
